The heading loop can take 0.19 s of delay before it oscillates, so Figure 1’s 0.1 s run settles and the 0.25 s run grows, because a delay T_d subtracts ω times T_d of phase and at 2.08 rad/s, where the loop’s gain is 1, 0.19 s uses up all of its 22.6° phase margin; without delay its gains can grow without limit, with 0.1 s of delay 13.4 times.
The loop transfer function (G-3035)Loop transfer function L(s): The product of every transfer function a signal meets once round a feedback loop, such as controller times plant times delay, L = C G e^{-sT_d}; the closed loop’s poles are where L(s) = -1, so stability and the margins are read off L alone. Explained in §2.1 Cutting the loop open. L, the product of everything a signal meets once round the loop, decides the closed loop’s poles, because they are the points where L = -1, the critical point (G-3036)Critical point: The point −1 of the complex plane: wherever the loop transfer function equals −1 the closed loop has a pole, so the Nyquist criterion counts turns round it and the margins measure the distance to it. Explained in §2.2 Where the closed loop has its poles..
A sine of 2.08 rad/s that comes back once round the loop exactly as it went in, gain 1 and phase −180°, keeps itself going, so L(j\omega) = -1 is the edge of stability.
The argument principle (G-3037)Argument principle: The rule that when a point walks clockwise once round a closed path, the image of a transfer function turns clockwise round 0 once for every zero inside minus once for every pole inside, N = Z - P; it lets us count poles in a region without solving for them. Explained in §3.2 Walking round a closed path. counts zeros minus poles inside a closed walk by the encirclements (G-3038)Encirclement (winding number): One full turn of a closed curve round a point; the winding number is the net count, clockwise minus counter-clockwise, which the Nyquist criterion reads off the Nyquist plot round −1. Explained in §3.2 Walking round a closed path. of the image, because each zero inside turns the image once clockwise and each pole once counter-clockwise.
The Nyquist contour (G-3039)Nyquist contour: The closed path, walked clockwise, up the whole imaginary axis and back round a huge half circle, with tiny half circles round any poles on the axis, so it encloses the right half-plane where unstable poles would lie. Explained in §4.1 The Nyquist contour: a walk round the right half-plane. walks round the whole right half-plane, stepping round the double pole at 0, and the Nyquist plot (G-3040)Nyquist plot: The loop transfer function drawn as the Nyquist contour is walked: mostly the frequency response, one point per frequency with the gain as length and the phase as angle, plus its mirror image; its turns round −1 decide closed-loop stability. Explained in §4.2 The Nyquist plot of the heading loop. is L drawn along it, so the Nyquist stability criterion (G-3041)Nyquist stability criterion: The test Z = N + P: the closed loop has Z poles in the right half-plane, where N is the number of clockwise turns of the Nyquist plot round −1 and P the number of the loop transfer function’s poles there; stable when Z = 0, delays and open-loop-unstable plants included. Explained in §4.3 Counting turns round the critical point., Z = N + P, counts the closed loop’s unstable poles: 0 for our 0.1 s loop, 2 for the 0.25 s loop.
A Bode-plot reading misleads when L has poles in the right half-plane: the balancing robot shows a gain of 2 at −180° yet is stable, because its one unstable pole of L is balanced by one counter-clockwise turn round −1.
The gain margin (G-3044)Gain margin: How many times the loop gain can grow before the closed loop becomes unstable, 1/\lvert L(j\omega_{pc})\rvert (often in dB); it says how far the Nyquist plot’s crossing of the negative real axis is from −1. Explained in §6.1 Gain margin., read at the phase crossover frequency (G-3043)Phase crossover frequency \omega_{pc}: The frequency at which the loop transfer function’s phase is −180°, where the Nyquist plot crosses the negative real axis; the gain margin is read there. Explained in §6.1 Gain margin., is unlimited for the delay-free loop and 13.4 with 0.1 s, because the delay alone bends the phase down to −180°.
The phase margin (G-3045)Phase margin: How much extra phase lag the loop can take at its gain crossover frequency before the closed loop becomes unstable, 180° + \angle L(j\omega_{gc}); typical designs keep 30° to 60°. Explained in §6.2 Phase margin., read at the gain crossover frequency (G-3042)Gain crossover frequency \omega_{gc}: The frequency at which the loop transfer function’s gain is 1 (0 dB), such as 2.08 rad/s for the robot’s heading loop; the phase margin and the delay margin are read there. Explained in §2.3 A sine that comes back unchanged., is 22.6°, below the usual 30° to 60°, so the loop has little room for any extra lag, such as a filter or a slower computer; RO-060’s tuned gains have 76.4°.
The delay margin (G-3046)Delay margin: The smallest extra time delay that makes the closed loop unstable, the phase margin in radians divided by the gain crossover frequency (0.19 s for the robot’s heading loop); it says how late sensor readings or commands may arrive. Explained in §6.3 Delay margin. is the phase margin divided by the gain crossover frequency, 0.19 s for our loop and 0.458 s for RO-060’s tuned loop, so phase margin buys tolerance of delay while a higher crossover spends it faster; PX4 cuts filter delay to raise its gains, and Autoware predicts across its 0.17 s actuator delay.

The robot can now say how much gain, phase lag or delay its heading loop can take, and check stability even for a robot that falls without control. Still missing: each margin measures one direction away from −1, so a combined change can break a loop with good margins, and nothing yet says how the loop treats disturbances and sensor noise; the sensitivity functionsSensitivity function S: The transfer function S = 1/(1 + L) of a feedback loop with loop transfer function L: it carries a disturbance at the output to the output, so the loop shrinks disturbances where \vert S\vert &lt; 1, and its gain is one over the distance from −1 to the Nyquist curve. Explained in: Sensitivity, Robustness and Loop Shaping, §2. How near the loop passes to the critical point: the sensitivity function measure both.
