The PI loop failed in six ways on the robot, and each now has a fix: the lag of 10 cm becomes 0 with feedforward, the wheel’s swing of 0.023 m/s becomes under 0.00001 m/s with a resonant term, the 1.04 m/s after holding becomes 0.515 m/s with clamping, the 4 N on the dock becomes 2 N with force control, the 11 N jump becomes at most 3.10 N with a rate limiter, and the carpet’s 11.1° becomes 0.21° with a cascade, because each fix removes the cause the opening named.
Feedforward (G-3070)Feedforward: A control force computed from the plan and the robot’s model, such as mass times the planned acceleration plus drag (m a_r + b v_r), and sent before any error appears; it lets the robot follow a changing plan without first falling behind, while feedback removes what the model gets wrong. Explained in §2.2 Feedforward from the robot’s model. sends the force the plan needs, mass times acceleration plus drag, before any error, so the robot no longer waits for an error to move; feedback still has to remove what the model gets wrong, as on carpet.
Integral action in state feedback (G-3073)Integral action in state feedback: Adding the running total of the error, z with dz/dt = r - v, as an extra state of the controller and feeding it back; a settled loop then has zero error for any model and any constant disturbance, because z stops changing only when the error is zero. Explained in §3.2 An integral state removes the error. adds the running total of the error as a state, so a settled loop has zero error for any model and any constant disturbance, which is why the PI held 0.5 m/s on the ramp.
The internal model principle (G-3074)Internal model principle: The rule that a controller can cancel a disturbance, or follow a reference, with zero error only if it contains a model of that signal: an integrator for a constant, an oscillator at the same frequency for a sine. Explained in §3.3 The internal model principle. says the controller must contain a model of what it cancels, so a resonant term (G-3075)Resonant term: A controller part that oscillates by itself at a chosen frequency and is driven by the error; added to a PI, it cancels a disturbance at that frequency, such as an out-of-round wheel’s push once per turn, because it can keep producing the cancelling wave while the error is zero. Explained in §3.3 The internal model principle. at the wheel’s 1 rad/s cancels the wheel’s push where the integral could not.
Actuator saturation (G-3076)Actuator saturation: An actuator stuck at its limit while the command asks for more, such as a drive giving its 4 N when 25.9 N is asked; while it lasts the force no longer depends on the error, so the feedback loop is broken. Explained in §4.1 The actuator cannot follow every command. breaks the loop, and integrator windup (G-3077)Integrator windup: The integral term growing while the actuator is saturated, because the error it cannot remove persists, and then keeping the actuator saturated after the error changes sign; it causes large overshoot, such as 1.04 m/s after a robot held for 3 s is let go. Explained in §4.2 How the integral winds up. follows because the integral keeps growing on an error the drive cannot remove; the overshoot comes from unwinding it.
Clamping (G-3079)Clamping (conditional integration): Anti-windup that stops the integration while the controller’s output is saturated and the error has the same sign as the command; it keeps the integral from piling up, and some sources use the same word for limiting the integral term’s value. Explained in §5.1 Clamping: stop integrating while saturated. stops the integration while the command is saturated and the error pushes further, and back-calculation (G-3080)Back-calculation: Anti-windup that feeds the gap between the force the actuator gives and the command, u - c, into the integrator through a gain k_{aw}; it pulls the command back towards the limit while saturated and does nothing otherwise. Explained in §5.2 Back-calculation: feed the shortfall back. feeds the shortfall into the integrator; both are anti-windup (G-3078)Anti-windup: Any change to a controller with integral action that keeps the integral from growing while the actuator is saturated, so the command leaves the limit as soon as the error changes sign; clamping and back-calculation are the two common kinds. Explained in §5. Anti-windup: clamping and back-calculation., and PX4, Apollo and ros2_control offer both.
