A velocity smoother (G-3085)Velocity smoother: A node between the planner and the robot’s base that clamps each velocity command to the speed limits, limits its rate of change and applies a deadband, such as Nav2’s nav2_velocity_smoother; it turns jumpy planner output into commands the drive can follow. Explained in §7.2 Deadband: send nothing the motors cannot do. with a rate limiter (G-3083)Rate limiter: A block that lets a signal change by at most a set amount per second, such as a speed command by 0.25 m/s every second (0.0125 m/s per 20 Hz cycle); it keeps commands within what the actuator can follow, so they do not saturate it. Explained in §7.1 Rate limits: never ask for more than the drive can give. and a deadband (G-3084)Deadband: A band of small values around zero inside which a command is replaced by 0, such as speed commands under 0.02 m/s; it keeps the robot from being asked for motions its motors cannot start. Explained in §7.2 Deadband: send nothing the motors cannot do. keeps commands within what the drive can follow, so saturation and windup are not invited in the first place.
