1. Overview



                  Key point 



A PID controller’s three numbers are found by experiments on the robot, either by raising the gain until the loop oscillates (Ziegler–Nichols) or by fitting a small model to one bump test. Practice then changes the textbook PID in three places, so that command steps and sensor noise do not jerk the motors.


The robot of this chapter can follow speed and heading commands. Its PID controller (G-2965)PID controller: A controller whose command is the sum of a proportional, an integral and a derivative part of the error, K_p e + K_i (integral of e) + K_d de/dt; the commonest feedback controller, it reacts to the error now, its past and its trend; with K_d = 0 it is a PI controller, with K_i = 0 a PD controller. Explained in: PD and PID Control, §5.2 Add the error up adds three actions on the error: one proportional to it, one to its accumulated area, one to its rate of change. RO-066 added feedforward (G-3070)Feedforward: A control force computed from the plan and the robot’s model, such as mass times the planned acceleration plus drag (m a_r + b v_r), and sent before any error appears; it lets the robot follow a changing plan without first falling behind, while feedback removes what the model gets wrong. Explained in: Feedforward, Integral Action, Cascaded Loops and Windup, §2.2 Feedforward from the robot’s model, the force the plan needs sent before any error; anti-windup (G-3078)Anti-windup: Any change to a controller with integral action that keeps the integral from growing while the actuator is saturated, so the command leaves the limit as soon as the error changes sign; clamping and back-calculation are the two common kinds. Explained in: Feedforward, Integral Action, Cascaded Loops and Windup, §5. Anti-windup: clamping and back-calculation, which stops the integral growing while the drive is at its limit; and cascade control (G-3086)Cascade control: Nesting feedback loops so that the outer loop’s output is the inner loop’s reference, such as a heading loop that commands the yaw rate for a gyro loop; it lets each loop be designed in turn, catches disturbances early and offers a place for limits. Explained in: Feedforward, Integral Action, Cascaded Loops and Windup, §8.1 One loop for the heading, or two, a fast inner loop under a slower outer one. Its speed loop is RO-060’s PI controller, with a proportional gain of 20 N per m/s and an integral gain of 10 N per m, chosen on the ideal model. Three things still go wrong on the real robot:

The gains. On a 1° rampExplained in: Feedforward, Integral Action, Cascaded Loops and Windup, §2.3 Feedforward plus feedback, gravity pulls the 8 kg robot back with 1.37 N. With those gains, the speed drops by 0.054 m/s and takes 5.2 s to come back within 0.005 m/s of 0.5 m/s. Larger gains would recover faster, but how large is safe? The ideal model says any gain is safe; the real robot rings when the gain is too high.
The command step. A heading correction of 0.1 rad (5.7°) makes RO-060’s heading PID ask for 2.85 N·m in one sample, 14 times the 0.2 N·m the motors can give, and the heading overshoots (G-2983)Overshoot (step response): How far a step response’s peak goes past its final value, as a percentage of the final value, such as 52.7 % when a 0.5 rad turn peaks at 0.763 rad; it says how far a reply swings past its target. Explained in: Reading a Controller’s Response: Step Response and Second-Order Systems, §2.1 Reading the four numbers, passes the command before it settles, by 21 percent.
The sensor noise. While the robot drives straight, every wheel-encoder tick makes the PID’s derivative action jump by 0.043 N·m, a fifth of the torque limit. A gyro has no ticks, but it carries the frame’s 10 Hz shaking into the derivative action as ±0.085 N·m.

Figure 1 draws the PID loop this Note tunes, with each change marked where it acts.

Figure 1: The PID loop of this Note. The reference r and the measurement y feed the P action, k_p times (beta r minus y), and the I action, k_i times the area of (r minus y); the D action, minus k_d times the rate of y_f, sees only the filtered measurement y_f. The three add up to the command u that drives the robot (its speed or its heading). Notes in grey name the section that sets each part: the gains (Sections 3 and 4), the weight beta and the derivative on the measurement (Section 5), the derivative filter and the notch filter (Section 6)
The sections answer the three problems in this order:

which three numbers a PID controller has, and the two standard ways to write them (Section 2);
the Ziegler–Nichols method (G-3093)Ziegler–Nichols method: A PID tuning recipe from one experiment: with only proportional control, raise the gain until the loop oscillates steadily, read the critical gain and period, and turn them into gains with a fixed table (PI: 0.45 k_c and T_c/1.2); quick, but it gives aggressive loops with small margins. Explained in §3.3 Raise the gain until the loop oscillates.: raise the gain until the speed loop oscillates, then use the oscillation’s gain and period (Section 3);
model-based tuning (G-3097)Model-based tuning: Choosing controller gains from a fitted model of the process, such as the FOTD model of a bump test fed into a rule like Feedback Systems eq. 11.10a; it uses more of the process’s behaviour than Ziegler–Nichols and gives calmer, more robust gains. Explained in §4.2 The model and the tuning rule.: measure one bump test, fit three numbers, and use a rule built for that model (Section 4);
the derivative kick (G-3098)Derivative kick: The spike in a PID’s output when its command jumps: the error jumps within one sample, so its computed rate, and the derivative action, become huge (2.85 N·m against a 0.2 N·m limit for a 0.1 rad heading step); avoided by taking the derivative of the measurement. Explained in §5.2 Derivative kick. and set-point weighting (G-3100)Set-point weighting: Letting the proportional and derivative actions see only fractions \beta and \gamma of the command, while the integral keeps the full error; it softens the response to command steps (overshoot 22.5 to 1.6 percent with \beta = 0.5) without changing the response to disturbances. Explained in §5.3 Set-point weighting., which stop a command step from jerking the motors (Section 5);
the derivative filter (G-3101)Derivative filter: A first-order low-pass on the PID’s derivative path with time constant T_f = T_d/N, N from 5 to 20; it smooths sensor noise such as encoder ticks before the derivative amplifies it, at the cost of some phase margin. Explained in §6.1 The derivative filter. and the notch filter (G-3102)Notch filter: A filter that blocks a narrow band around one frequency and passes the rest, (s^2 + \omega_0^2)/(s^2 + (\omega_0/Q)s + \omega_0^2); it removes a known vibration, such as a frame’s 10 Hz bending seen by the gyro, with far less lag at the loop’s own frequencies than a low-pass. Explained in §6.2 The notch filter., which keep the encoder steps and the 10 Hz shaking out of the derivative action (Section 6).

2. The PID forms: which numbers we tune



                  Key point 



Tuning starts by naming the numbers to tune. The same ideal PID controller (G-3090)Ideal PID controller: The textbook PID law, u = k_p e + k_i \int e\thinspace dt + k_d\thinspace de/dt, with nothing added: no output limits, no filters, every action on the error; a real controller adds set-point weights, derivative on measurement, filters and anti-windup to it. Explained in §2.1 The ideal PID controller in parallel form. can be written with three gains (the parallel form, G-3091)Parallel form (PID): The PID controller written with one gain per action, k_p, k_i and k_d, whose three results add up, such as 0.2, 0.05 and 0.283 for the robot’s heading loop (RO-060’s gains); most robot software (ros2_control, Autoware, Apollo) takes its gains this way. Explained in §2.1 The ideal PID controller in parallel form. or with one gain and two times (the standard form, G-3092)Standard form (PID): The PID controller written as one gain times a bracket, k_p(e + \int e\thinspace dt/T_i + T_d\thinspace de/dt), with the integral time T_i and derivative time T_d; changing k_p alone scales all three actions and keeps their timing. Explained in §2.2 The standard form: one gain and two times.; the two times compare directly with how fast the robot moves.


2.1 The ideal PID controller in parallel form
All three problems of the Overview are about the numbers inside the PID controller, so we first write them down. The PID controller (G-2965)PID controller: A controller whose command is the sum of a proportional, an integral and a derivative part of the error, K_p e + K_i (integral of e) + K_d de/dt; the commonest feedback controller, it reacts to the error now, its past and its trend; with K_d = 0 it is a PI controller, with K_i = 0 a PD controller. Explained in: PD and PID Control, §5.2 Add the error up looks at the error, the command minus the measurement, and adds three actions: one proportional to the error, one to the area under the error so far, one to the error’s rate of change, its derivative (G-595)Derivative: The slope of a function at a point: how fast the output changes as the input changes there. It is zero at the lowest point of a smooth curve, which is how training finds a minimum. Explained in: Derivatives of One Variable, §4.1 From secant to tangent. Written with one gain per action, this is the ideal PID controller (G-3090)Ideal PID controller: The textbook PID law, u = k_p e + k_i \int e\thinspace dt + k_d\thinspace de/dt, with nothing added: no output limits, no filters, every action on the error; a real controller adds set-point weights, derivative on measurement, filters and anti-windup to it. Explained in §2.1 The ideal PID controller in parallel form. in its parallel form (G-3091)Parallel form (PID): The PID controller written with one gain per action, k_p, k_i and k_d, whose three results add up, such as 0.2, 0.05 and 0.283 for the robot’s heading loop (RO-060’s gains); most robot software (ros2_control, Autoware, Apollo) takes its gains this way. Explained in §2.1 The ideal PID controller in parallel form. (FBS §11.1, eq. 11.1). “Ideal” means the textbook law with nothing added: no limits and no filters. Sections 5 and 6 add what a real robot needs.
For the robot’s heading loop, which turns the robot by a torque, with RO-060’s gains:

e is the error, the heading command minus the measured heading, in rad;
u is the controller’s output, the torque it asks the motors for, in N·m (the motors give at most 0.2 N·m either way);
k_p is the proportional gain (G-2959)Proportional gain: The factor K_p by which a proportional controller multiplies the error, such as 0.2 N m per rad or 20 N per m/s; a larger gain corrects harder, and for an undamped plant swings faster. Explained in: PD and PID Control, §3.1 The rule, and why it overshoots, 0.2 N·m per rad;
k_i is the integral gain (G-2964)Integral gain: The factor K_i by which integral action multiplies the error’s running sum, such as 0.05 N m per rad s; larger values remove a steady error sooner but swing more, and above a limit make the loop unstable. Explained in: PD and PID Control, §5.2 Add the error up, 0.05 N·m per rad·s, which multiplies the area under the error;
k_d is the derivative gain (G-2961)Derivative gain: The factor K_d by which derivative action multiplies the error’s rate of change, such as 0.283 N m per rad/s; too little leaves a swing, too much makes the response sluggish. Explained in: PD and PID Control, §4.1 Look at how fast the error shrinks, 0.283 N·m·s per rad, which multiplies the error’s rate of change.

u = k_p\thinspace e + k_i \int_0^t e\thinspace dt + k_d\thinspace\frac{de}{dt}
The integral sign, \int, with 0 below and t above, means the area under the error curve from time 0 to now, t. An error of 0.1 rad held for 2 s has the area:
0.1 \times 2 = 0.2 \text{ rad·s}
Figure 2 (top) draws this form: the error goes down three branches, each with its own gain, and the three results add up.
2.2 The standard form: one gain and two times
Three separate gains hide how the actions compare in time. The same controller can be written with one overall gain and two times (FBS §11.1):

k_p is the same proportional gain, 0.2 N·m per rad, now multiplying all three actions;
T_i is the integral time (G-3103)Integral time T_i: The proportional gain over the integral gain, T_i = k_p/k_i, in seconds: the time a constant error takes to make the integral action as large as the proportional action (2 s for k_p = 20, k_i = 10), so a short one makes the integral take over quickly. Explained in §2.2 The standard form: one gain and two times., 4 s for the heading PID;
T_d is the derivative time (G-3104)Derivative time T_d: The derivative gain over the proportional gain, T_d = k_d/k_p, in seconds: how far ahead the derivative action predicts the error along a straight line, since it applies now the change the proportional action will see T_d later. Explained in §2.2 The standard form: one gain and two times., 1.415 s for the heading PID.

u = k_p\Big(e + \frac{1}{T_i}\int_0^t e\thinspace dt + T_d\thinspace\frac{de}{dt}\Big)
The form with one gain and two times is the standard form (G-3092)Standard form (PID): The PID controller written as one gain times a bracket, k_p(e + \int e\thinspace dt/T_i + T_d\thinspace de/dt), with the integral time T_i and derivative time T_d; changing k_p alone scales all three actions and keeps their timing. Explained in §2.2 The standard form: one gain and two times. (FBS §11.1), Figure 2 (bottom). Multiplying out the bracket gives back the parallel form, so the two gains of the parallel form are, our numbers first:
k_i = 0.2 / 4 = 0.05
k_d = 0.2 \times 1.415 = 0.283
and in symbols:
k_i = k_p / T_i
k_d = k_p\thinspace T_d
The speed PI controller has no derivative action; with its gains of 20 and 10, its integral time is:
T_i = 20 / 10 = 2 \text{ s}

Figure 2: One ideal PID controller drawn two ways, with the heading PID’s numbers. Top, parallel form: the error e goes through three blocks, k_p times e (k_p = 0.2), k_i times the area of e (k_i = 0.05) and k_d times the rate of e (k_d = 0.283), and the three add up to u. Bottom, standard form: the three blocks are 1 times e, 1/T_i times the area of e (T_i = 4 s) and T_d times the rate of e (T_d = 1.415 s); their sum is multiplied by one gain, k_p = 0.2. The note at the bottom states the link: k_i = k_p/T_i and k_d = k_p T_d
What the two times mean. Each time is the time over which one action catches up with the proportional action.
The integral time. Hold a speed error of 0.1 m/s on the speed PI controller. The proportional action and the integral action’s growth per second are:
20 \times 0.1 = 2 \text{ N}
10 \times 0.1 = 1 \text{ N per s}
After T_i = 2 s the integral action has added:
1 \times 2 = 2 \text{ N}
as much as the proportional action. A short integral time means the integral action takes over quickly.
The derivative time. Let the heading error fall steadily by 0.1 rad/s. The derivative action is:
0.283 \times (-0.1) = -0.0283 \text{ N·m}
In T_d = 1.415 s the error falls by 0.1415 rad, which changes the proportional action by:
0.2 \times (-0.1415) = -0.0283 \text{ N·m}
the same amount. The derivative action applies now the change the proportional action will see T_d seconds later, a prediction by a straight line (FBS §11.1).
Both times are measured in seconds, so they compare directly with the robot’s own times, such as the speed plant’s time constant of 4 s. Changing k_p alone scales all three actions together and keeps their timing. The PX4 tuning guide gives this as the reason for the standard form: a new drone can start from the gains of a similar one and adjust the one overall gain (PX4 tuning guide).
In real stacks. PX4’s rate controller offers both forms at once: its parameters MC_ROLLRATE_P, I, D and an overall gain MC_ROLLRATE_K; setting K = 1 gives the parallel form, setting P = 1 the standard form (PX4 tuning guide). The PID class of ros2_control (pid.hpp in control_toolbox) and the speed controllers of Autoware and Apollo take three parallel gains named p, i, d or kp, ki, kd. The numbers are now named, but nothing yet says which values to give them.
3. Ziegler–Nichols: tuning from one experiment



                  Key point 



The Ziegler–Nichols method raises the proportional gain until the loop oscillates steadily, reads that critical gain and critical period (G-3094)Critical gain and critical period: The proportional gain k_c at which a loop oscillates without growing or dying, and the period T_c of that oscillation (200 N per m/s and 0.40 s for the robot’s speed loop); they mark the point where the loop’s lags add up to half a period, and the Ziegler–Nichols table uses them. Also called ultimate gain and period. Explained in §3.3 Raise the gain until the loop oscillates., and turns them into PID gains by a table. On the robot’s speed loop it gives fast but fragile gains.


3.1 Why the ideal speed model never oscillates
The first problem of the Overview asks how large the speed gains can be before the robot rings. On the ideal model the answer is: any size. Under proportional control alone, the speed plant of RO-059 is a first-order system. With:

m the robot’s mass, 8 kg;
b the rolling drag, 2 N·s/m;
v the speed, in m/s, and r the commanded speed, 0.5 m/s;
k_p the proportional gain, in N per m/s;

the drive force, the gain times the speed error, minus the drag accelerates the mass:
m\thinspace\frac{dv}{dt} = k_p(r - v) - b\thinspace v
m\thinspace\frac{dv}{dt} = k_p\thinspace r - (k_p + b)\thinspace v
The second line has the shape of a first-order system (G-2987)First-order system: A system whose output follows a differential equation with only a first derivative, such as the robot’s speed under P control; it closes the same fraction of its remaining gap in every time constant and can never overshoot. Explained in: Reading a Controller’s Response: Step Response and Second-Order Systems, §4.1 The time constant: the speed approaches its final value along a curve that never overshoots, with the time constant (G-2988)Time constant: The time a first-order system takes to close 63 % of the gap to its final value, m/(K_v + b) = 1 s for the robot’s speed loop; it fixes the rise time (about 2.2 time constants) and the 2 % settling time (about 4). Explained in: Reading a Controller’s Response: Step Response and Second-Order Systems, §4.1 The time constant:
\frac{m}{k_p + b}
For two gains:
k_p = 20: \quad 8 / 22 = 0.36 \text{ s}
k_p = 200: \quad 8 / 202 = 0.040 \text{ s}
A larger gain only makes the curve steeper. The heading plant is no better guide: with proportional control alone nothing damps its turn, its damping ratio (G-2992)Damping ratio: A second-order system’s damping compared with the damping that just stops it from swinging, \zeta = K_d / (2\sqrt{K_p I}), 0.2 for D gain 0.08 and 1 for 0.4; it alone sets the shape of the reply and its overshoot. Explained in: Reading a Controller’s Response: Step Response and Second-Order Systems, §5.1 Natural frequency and damping ratio, the number that sets how fast a swing dies out, is 0 at every gain, so it swings forever and only the period changes; no single gain stands out. A tuning experiment needs a loop that is calm at a low gain and starts to oscillate only above some gain. The real speed loop is such a loop, because of a detail the ideal model left out.
3.2 The measured speed arrives late
The controller does not see the true speed. The wheel encoders count ticks, each 0.307 mm of wheel travel (Section 6.1 works it out). Within one sample of 0.01 s, one tick more or less changes the computed speed by:
0.000307 / 0.01 = 0.031 \text{ m/s}
A jump of 0.031 m/s is 6 percent of 0.5 m/s, so the speed computed from one sample is jumpy. The controller therefore uses the moving average (G-2267)Moving average: The list of averages a sliding window gives: each output is the mean of the numbers under the window, so sharp jumps are smoothed out. Explained in: The Convolution Operation, §4.1 A moving average of the last 20 samples, 0.2 s of wheel speeds, where one tick changes the average by only:
0.000307 / 0.2 = 0.0015 \text{ m/s}
ros2_control’s diff_drive_controller does the same with a rolling mean of the last 10 samples by default (its parameter velocity_rolling_window_size). Figure 3 slides that 0.2 s window along the speed while the robot speeds up at 0.25 m/s². Watch the orange dot, the average the controller reads now: it always sits level with the true speed of a moment earlier.

Figure 3: The robot speeds up at 0.25 m/s^2 (blue line, the true speed). Each frame slides the 0.2 s window (orange band) one step on; the 20 samples inside it are the blue dots, and their average is the orange dot, which the controller reads at that moment. The dotted line runs back from the orange dot to the time when the true speed had that value. The orange line, the measured speed, starts at 0 and then runs parallel to the true speed, 0.024 m/s below it. The last frame states it: the measured speed is the true speed of 0.095 s ago
Why the average is late. The window holds the speeds at the times t, t - 0.01, …, t - 0.19 s. While the speed rises along a straight line, the average of the 20 samples is the speed at the window’s middle. With:

n the number of samples in the window, 20;
h the sample period (G-3106)Sample period and sample rate: The time T between two samples, and its inverse, the number of samples per second f_s = 1/T in hertz, such as 0.01 s and 100 Hz; they set how often a digital controller sees the robot and acts. Explained in: Digital Control: Sampling a Continuous Controller, §2. What the computer sees: sampling, the time between two readings, 0.01 s;

the middle lies this far back, our numbers first:
\text{lag} = \frac{20 - 1}{2} \times 0.01
= 0.095 \text{ s}
and in symbols:
\text{lag} = \frac{n - 1}{2}\thinspace h
At 0.25 m/s², 0.095 s of lag is a speed gap of:
0.25 \times 0.095 = 0.024 \text{ m/s}
So the controller acts on a speed about 0.1 s old, like a driver steering by where the car was a moment ago. A fast correction, made on old news, overshoots, and a high gain makes fast corrections. The experiment of Section 3.3 finds the gain at which this turns into a steady oscillation.
3.3 Raise the gain until the loop oscillates
Ziegler and Nichols’s frequency-response experiment works on the running loop (FBS §11.3): turn off the integral and derivative actions, then raise the proportional gain step by step until the output oscillates steadily. We run it on the robot cruising at 0.5 m/s, with the speed loop of RO-059Explained in: Feedback Basics: Open and Closed Loop and the Block Diagram, §3.1 Measure, compare, correct: the drive force is the 1 N that holds 0.5 m/s against the drag, plus k_p times the error, and k_p is raised from its 20 N per m/s. At t = 0.2 s the speed command steps up by only 0.01 m/s, so that even at a gain of 200 the force stays inside the ±4 N limit (between −0.54 and 3.0 N); a saturated drive would spoil the reading.
Figure 4 plays four runs. Watch the ringing after the step last longer with each larger gain, until at 200 N per m/s it neither grows nor dies.

Figure 4: The Ziegler–Nichols experiment on the speed loop, one run per gain: k_p = 50, 100, 150 and 200 N per m/s, proportional control only, the command stepping from 0.5 to 0.51 m/s (dashed line) at t = 0.2 s. Each run plays in steps and stays faint once the next starts. Top: the measured speed; bottom: the drive force, with the ±4 N limit dotted. At 50 the speed settles; at 100 and 150 it rings, longer at 150; at 200 the swings keep the same size. The last frame marks two neighbouring peaks of the 200 run, one critical period T_c = 0.40 s apart
The gain at which the oscillation neither grows nor dies is the critical gain, and its period is the critical period (G-3094)Critical gain and critical period: The proportional gain k_c at which a loop oscillates without growing or dying, and the period T_c of that oscillation (200 N per m/s and 0.40 s for the robot’s speed loop); they mark the point where the loop’s lags add up to half a period, and the Ziegler–Nichols table uses them. Also called ultimate gain and period. Explained in §3.3 Raise the gain until the loop oscillates. (FBS §11.3), here:

k_c is the critical gain, 200 N per m/s;
T_c is the critical period, 0.40 s.

At a gain of 220 the swings grow from one period to the next (notebook): above k_c the loop is unstable.
Why it oscillates there. A steady oscillation goes round the loop and comes back the same size and in step with itself. The sign flip of the error, r - y, already turns it upside down once, so the drive and the averaging together must delay it by half a period, 180°, and leave its size unchanged. At the critical frequency (one swing per 0.396 s, read as 0.40 s in Figure 4) the delays are (notebook):

the drive’s own lag, the plant’s response to a force swing: 89.1°;
the 20-sample average, 0.095 s late: 86.4°;
the controller’s 0.01 s steps, which act like a delay of half a sampleExplained in: Digital Control: Sampling a Continuous Controller, §3.2 The hold delays by half a sample, 0.005 s: the remaining 4.5°.

At the same frequency the drive and the average shrink a swing of the force to 1/200.7 of its size in the measured speed, so a gain of 200.7 brings it back to exactly its starting size; Figure 4 reads it as 200. In the language of the Nyquist criterion (G-3041)Nyquist stability criterion: The test Z = N + P: the closed loop has Z poles in the right half-plane, where N is the number of clockwise turns of the Nyquist plot round −1 and P the number of the loop transfer function’s poles there; stable when Z = 0, delays and open-loop-unstable plants included. Explained in: The Nyquist Criterion and Stability Margins, §4.3 Counting turns round the critical point, the loop’s curve passes through the critical point (G-3036)Critical point: The point −1 of the complex plane: wherever the loop transfer function equals −1 the closed loop has a pole, so the Nyquist criterion counts turns round it and the margins measure the distance to it. Explained in: The Nyquist Criterion and Stability Margins, §2.2 Where the closed loop has its poles, −1, at this frequency (FBS §11.3).
3.4 The Ziegler–Nichols rules and their price
Ziegler and Nichols turned the two numbers into controller settings by a table (FBS Table 11.1b). They built it in the 1940s from simulations of many processes, each tuned by hand (FBS §11.3):





























Controllerk_pT_iT_dP0.5\thinspace k_cPI0.45\thinspace k_cT_c / 1.2PID0.6\thinspace k_cT_c / 2T_c / 8
The speed loop is RO-060’s PI controller, so with k_c = 200 N per m/s and T_c = 0.40 s:
k_p = 0.45 \times 200 = 90
T_i = 0.40 / 1.2 = 0.333 \text{ s}
k_i = 90 / 0.333 = 270
in N per m/s and N per m, 4.5 and 27 times the speed PI’s 20 and 10. Figure 6 in Section 4.3 runs them on the 1° ramp: the speed drops by only 0.021 m/s and is back within 0.005 m/s after 0.8 s. The price shows elsewhere:

a step of the speed command by 0.05 m/s overshoots by 83 percent and drives the force to its 4 N limit;
the loop has a phase margin (G-3045)Phase margin: How much extra phase lag the loop can take at its gain crossover frequency before the closed loop becomes unstable, 180° + \angle L(j\omega_{gc}); typical designs keep 30° to 60°. Explained in: The Nyquist Criterion and Stability Margins, §6.2 Phase margin, the extra phase lag it can take at its crossover before it oscillates, of only 18°, and its delay margin (G-3046)Delay margin: The smallest extra time delay that makes the closed loop unstable, the phase margin in radians divided by the gain crossover frequency (0.19 s for the robot’s heading loop); it says how late sensor readings or commands may arrive. Explained in: The Nyquist Criterion and Stability Margins, §6.3 Delay margin, the extra delay it can take, is 0.032 s; with the speed measured 0.04 s later still, it oscillates (Figure 6, bottom).

Feedback Systems names the two drawbacks of the rules: they use too little information about the process, and the loops they give lack robustness (FBS §11.3). The experiment saw only one point of the loop, the 180° point. The next section measures the whole response once and lets a rule use more of it.
4. Model-based tuning: a bump test and a small model



                  Key point 



One bump test (G-3095)Bump test: An open-loop experiment: hold the process steady, step its input once (the drive force from 0 to 1 N) and record the output until it settles; its curve gives the gain, the apparent delay and the time constant used to tune a controller. Explained in §4.1 The bump test., a step of the drive force with the loop open, gives three numbers: a gain, a delay and a time constant, the first-order-plus-time-delay model (G-3096)First-order-plus-time-delay (FOTD) model: A three-number model of a step response: nothing happens for a delay \tau, then the output rises like a first-order system with gain K and time constant T (0.5 m/s per N, 0.094 s and 4.00 s for the robot); simple tuning rules are written for it. Also called first order plus dead time. Explained in §4.2 The model and the tuning rule.. A tuning rule built for that model gives speed gains that cut the ramp’s speed drop from 0.054 to 0.039 m/s and keep a delay margin of 0.27 s.


4.1 The bump test
The Ziegler–Nichols gains were fast but fragile, because one oscillation is little information. A bump test records the robot’s whole response to one step (FBS §11.3): with the controller off, the drive force steps from 0 to 1 N and stays there, and we record the measured speed until it settles. Figure 5 plays the test. Watch the top panel for the final speed and the bottom panel, the first second enlarged, for the late start.

Figure 5: The bump test. The robot stands still; at t = 0 the drive force steps to 1 N and stays there. Top, the whole test over 30 s: the measured speed (orange) rises along the true speed (blue dots) and settles at 0.5 m/s. Bottom, the first second enlarged: the measured speed starts late. The later frames read the test: the steepest tangent (red, 0.122 m/s^2 at t = 0.2 s) crosses the time axis at tau = 0.094 s and the speed axis at −a = −0.0115 m/s; the speed settles at K = 0.5 m/s per N; it reaches 63 percent of that at T63 = 4.09 s. The last frame holds K = 0.5 m/s per N, tau = 0.094 s and T = T63 − tau = 4.00 s
Three readings come off the test (FBS §11.3, Fig. 11.7a):

K is the gain, the final speed per newton of drive force: 0.5 m/s per N;
\tau is the apparent delay, where the steepest tangent of the response crosses the time axis: 0.094 s;
T_{63} is the time at which the speed has covered 63 percent of its rise: 4.09 s.

The 63 percent mark is one time constant (G-2988)Time constant: The time a first-order system takes to close 63 % of the gap to its final value, m/(K_v + b) = 1 s for the robot’s speed loop; it fixes the rise time (about 2.2 time constants) and the 2 % settling time (about 4). Explained in: Reading a Controller’s Response: Step Response and Second-Order Systems, §4.1 The time constant of a first-order system. Here the response starts late by \tau, so the time constant T is:
T = 4.09 - 0.094 = 4.00 \text{ s}
T = T_{63} - \tau
The three numbers agree with the physics of the robot: the gain K is one over the drag, 0.5 m/s per N; the time constant T is the mass over the drag, 4 s; the delay \tau is close to the averaging lag of Section 3.2, 0.095 s. The test needs patience, though: the speed must settle before K can be read, 30 s here, where the Ziegler–Nichols experiment needed a few seconds per gain (FBS §11.3).
4.2 The model and the tuning rule
The three numbers describe the response as a delay followed by a first-order rise, the first-order-plus-time-delay (FOTD) model (G-3096)First-order-plus-time-delay (FOTD) model: A three-number model of a step response: nothing happens for a delay \tau, then the output rises like a first-order system with gain K and time constant T (0.5 m/s per N, 0.094 s and 4.00 s for the robot); simple tuning rules are written for it. Also called first order plus dead time. Explained in §4.2 The model and the tuning rule. (FBS eq. 11.9), also called first order plus dead time. With:

F the size of the force step, 1 N;
t the time since the step, in s;
\Delta y the rise of the measured speed, in m/s;
K, \tau and T the gain, the delay and the time constant read off the bump test;
\exp the exponential function, \exp(x) = 2.718^x, so that \exp(-1) = 0.368;

the model says:
\Delta y = 0 \quad \text{for} \quad t &lt; \tau
\Delta y = K F\big(1 - \exp(-\tfrac{t - \tau}{T})\big)
\text{for} \quad t \ge \tau
One time constant after the delay, the bracket is:
1 - 0.368 = 0.632
the 63 percent mark of Section 4.1.
A delay that is small next to the time constant, as here (0.094 s against 4.00 s), makes a process lag dominated (FBS §11.3). For such a process, rules based on this model suit PI controllers; a PID needs a different method (FBS §11.3), and the speed loop is a PI controller anyway.
Tuning a controller from a model of the process is model-based tuning (G-3097)Model-based tuning: Choosing controller gains from a fitted model of the process, such as the FOTD model of a bump test fed into a rule like Feedback Systems eq. 11.10a; it uses more of the process’s behaviour than Ziegler–Nichols and gives calmer, more robust gains. Explained in §4.2 The model and the tuning rule.. Feedback Systems gives PI rules for the FOTD model that use all three numbers (FBS eq. 11.10a). With K = 0.5 m/s per N, \tau = 0.094 s and T = 4.00 s, the proportional gain, our numbers first:
0.15 \times 0.094 = 0.0141
0.35 \times 4.00 = 1.4
0.5 \times 0.094 = 0.047
k_p = (0.0141 + 1.4) / 0.047
= 1.4141 / 0.047 = 30.1
and in symbols:
k_p = \frac{0.15\thinspace\tau + 0.35\thinspace T}{K\tau}
The integral gain, our numbers first:
0.46 \times 0.094 = 0.04324
0.02 \times 4.00 = 0.08
0.5 \times 0.094^2 = 0.004418
k_i = (0.04324 + 0.08) / 0.004418
= 0.12324 / 0.004418 = 27.9
and in symbols:
k_i = \frac{0.46\thinspace\tau + 0.02\thinspace T}{K\tau^2}
The units work out to N per m/s and N per m. The rule’s proportional gain is a third of Ziegler–Nichols’s 90; Feedback Systems notes that such improved rules give lower gains than the original table (FBS §11.3).
The same test can also feed Ziegler and Nichols’s other experiment, their step-response method (FBS Table 11.1a). The step-response method uses only \tau and a, where the steepest tangent crosses the speed axis, 0.0115 m/s here. Its PI gains:
k_p = 0.9 / 0.0115 = 78.3
T_i = 0.094 / 0.3 = 0.313 \text{ s}
k_i = 78.3 / 0.313 = 250
close to the frequency method’s 90 and 270, and just as fragile.
