Gains are in N per m/s and N per m; the phase margin (G-3045)Phase margin: How much extra phase lag the loop can take at its gain crossover frequency before the closed loop becomes unstable, 180° + \angle L(j\omega_{gc}); typical designs keep 30° to 60°. Explained in: The Nyquist Criterion and Stability Margins, §6.2 Phase margin and the delay margin (G-3046)Delay margin: The smallest extra time delay that makes the closed loop unstable, the phase margin in radians divided by the gain crossover frequency (0.19 s for the robot’s heading loop); it says how late sensor readings or commands may arrive. Explained in: The Nyquist Criterion and Stability Margins, §6.3 Delay margin, how much extra lag and extra delay the loop tolerates (stability marginsExplained in: The Nyquist Criterion and Stability Margins, §6. Stability margins: how far from the critical point), are computed from the loop at 100 Hz (notebook).
The bump-test gains answer the first problem of the Overview. They cut the speed drop on the 1° ramp from 0.054 to 0.039 m/s and the recovery from 5.2 to 2.3 s, and they still tolerate 0.27 s of extra delay. Ziegler–Nichols recovers fastest, but 0.04 s of extra delay makes it oscillate. RO-060’s gains are the calmest and the slowest. Which pair to keep depends on what the robot needs, and the model-based rule sits in the useful middle. The step overshoot of every pair can still be lowered without touching its gains, by the weighting of Section 5.3.
In real stacks. The PX4 tuning guide tunes a drone by hand: it raises a gain by 20 to 30 percent per try, then by 5 to 10 percent for the final touches; it tunes the proportional and derivative gains first and the integral gain last; and it starts with the rate controller, the innermost loop, because that loop “affects all flight modes” (PX4 tuning guide). Tuning the inner loop of a cascadeExplained in: Feedforward, Integral Action, Cascaded Loops and Windup, §8. Cascaded loops: a fast inner loop under a slower outer loop first is the order Feedback Systems gives too (FBS §1.5). The speed loops of Autoware and Apollo are PI controllers too: their shipped parameters set the derivative gain to 0 (Autoware’s longitudinal controller: kp 1.0, ki 0.1, kd 0.0; Apollo’s controller_conf.pb.txt: kp 2.0, ki 0.3, kd 0.0 at low speed; files listed in Sources). The speed loop is now tuned; the heading loop, which needs its derivative action, still kicks.
5. Derivative kick and set-point weighting



                  Key point 



When the command jumps, the error jumps, and the derivative of a jump is huge: the derivative kick (G-3098)Derivative kick: The spike in a PID’s output when its command jumps: the error jumps within one sample, so its computed rate, and the derivative action, become huge (2.85 N·m against a 0.2 N·m limit for a 0.1 rad heading step); avoided by taking the derivative of the measurement. Explained in §5.2 Derivative kick.. Taking the derivative of the measurement instead, the derivative on measurement (G-3099)Derivative on measurement: Computing the D action from the rate of change of the measurement, -k_d\thinspace dy/dt, instead of the error’s; it equals the usual D action whenever the command is constant and ignores command jumps, so it removes the derivative kick. Explained in §5.2 Derivative kick., removes it; set-point weighting (G-3100)Set-point weighting: Letting the proportional and derivative actions see only fractions \beta and \gamma of the command, while the integral keeps the full error; it softens the response to command steps (overshoot 22.5 to 1.6 percent with \beta = 0.5) without changing the response to disturbances. Explained in §5.3 Set-point weighting. also lowers the proportional jump and cuts the heading’s overshoot from 22.5 to 1.6 percent.


5.1 The heading PID and its test
The second problem of the Overview is the heading loop. The heading plant has no friction to calm a turn, so its controller needs the derivative action (G-2960)Derivative action: A part of the command in proportion to the error’s rate of change, estimated from two readings in a row; it pushes against fast changes, so it brakes a turn before the target and damps swings, but it magnifies sensor noise. Explained in: PD and PID Control, §4.1 Look at how fast the error shrinks, which brakes the turn before the target. We use RO-060’s heading PID, the gains of Section 2: k_p = 0.2 N·m per rad, k_i = 0.05 N·m per rad·s, k_d = 0.283 N·m·s per rad; RO-060 built it from its PD controller (G-2962)PD control: Feedback with a proportional part and a derivative part, such as K_p e + K_d de/dt; it acts like a spring and a damper, so it can bring a frictionless joint or heading to its target without swinging. Explained in: PD and PID Control, §4.1 Look at how fast the error shrinks by adding the error upExplained in: PD and PID Control, §5.2 Add the error up, the integral action (G-2963)Integral action: A part of the command in proportion to the error’s running sum (its integral over time); the sum grows while any error remains, so it removes the error a steady load leaves, at the cost of overshoot and, if too large, instability. Explained in: PD and PID Control, §5.2 Add the error up. With the yaw inertia of 0.1 kg·m², these gains give the closed loop one pole (G-3000)Pole: A value of s where a transfer function’s denominator is zero, so the fraction blows up there, such as −2 (twice) for the PD heading loop; each pole adds a term e^(pt) to every reply, so its real part says fade or grow and its imaginary part how fast it swings. Explained in: Transfer Functions, Poles and Zeros, §3.1 Poles: where the fraction blows up, a rate at which a part of the response dies away, at −1.93 rad/s and a complex pair at −0.45 ± 0.24i rad/s (notebook): a gentle turn that takes several seconds to settle.
The test is a heading correction from the path tracker: at t = 0.5 s, the heading command steps from 0 to 0.1 rad (5.7°). The step is small, so the proportional action alone stays inside the 0.2 N·m limit:
0.2 \times 0.1 = 0.02 \text{ N·m}
Until Section 6 the measured heading is exact.
5.2 Derivative kick
The derivative action of Section 2.1 acts on the rate of change of the error. In the sample where the command steps, the error jumps by 0.1 rad within one 0.01 s sample. With:

\Delta e the error’s jump, 0.1 rad;
h the sample period, 0.01 s;
k_d the derivative gain, 0.283 N·m·s per rad;

the computed rate and the derivative action in that sample are:
\frac{\Delta e}{h} = \frac{0.1}{0.01} = 10 \text{ rad/s}
k_d \times 10 = 0.283 \times 10 = 2.83 \text{ N·m}
With the proportional action, the request is:
2.83 + 0.02 = 2.85 \text{ N·m}
2.85 / 0.2 = 14.25
14 times what the motors can give. A shorter sample would make it larger still; an ideal derivative of a jump is infinite (Beauregard). The spike is the derivative kick (G-3098)Derivative kick: The spike in a PID’s output when its command jumps: the error jumps within one sample, so its computed rate, and the derivative action, become huge (2.85 N·m against a 0.2 N·m limit for a 0.1 rad heading step); avoided by taking the derivative of the measurement. Explained in §5.2 Derivative kick. (Beauregard; FBS §11.5).
The fix. Between jumps the command stays put, so the rate of change of the error e is minus the rate of change of the measurement. With:

r the heading command, in rad;
y the measured heading, in rad;

\frac{de}{dt} = \frac{dr}{dt} - \frac{dy}{dt}
= -\frac{dy}{dt} \quad \text{while } r \text{ is constant}
So a derivative action computed from the measurement,
-k_d\thinspace\frac{dy}{dt}
equals the old one at every moment except the jump, where it ignores the command. Taking the derivative of the measurement instead of the error is the derivative on measurement (G-3099)Derivative on measurement: Computing the D action from the rate of change of the measurement, -k_d\thinspace dy/dt, instead of the error’s; it equals the usual D action whenever the command is constant and ignores command jumps, so it removes the derivative kick. Explained in §5.2 Derivative kick. (Beauregard; FBS §11.5). Figure 7 turns the robot with both. Watch the torque request at t = 0.5 s, then the two headings below.

Figure 7: Derivative kick on RO-060’s heading PID (k_p = 0.2, k_i = 0.05, k_d = 0.283). At t = 0.5 s the heading command steps from 0 to 0.1 rad (dashed line, bottom). Top: the torque request, with the 0.2 N m limit dotted. Red, derivative on the error: in the step’s sample the request spikes to 2.85 N m, far off the chart. Blue, derivative on the measurement: the request starts at 0.02 N m. Bottom: the two headings lie almost on top of each other (overshoot 21.1 and 22.5 percent). The last frame holds the whole 10 s
The heading barely changes (21.1 against 22.5 percent overshoot), because the motors clip the spike to 0.2 N·m for one sample. The derivative on measurement turns the robot the same way without sending the motors a 2.85 N·m command at every heading step (Beauregard). The overshoot, though, is still 22.5 percent.
5.3 Set-point weighting
The proportional action also reacts to the step: it jumps from 0 to 0.02 N·m at once. Feedback Systems lets each action see only a fraction of the command (FBS eq. 11.15):

\beta is the weight of the command in the proportional action, from 0 to 1;
\gamma is the weight of the command in the derivative action, normally 0 (FBS §11.5);
k_p, k_i, k_d, r and y are as above.

u = k_p(\beta r - y)
+ k_i \int_0^t (r - y)\thinspace dt
+ k_d\Big(\gamma\thinspace\frac{dr}{dt} - \frac{dy}{dt}\Big)
Letting each action see its own fraction of the command is set-point weighting (G-3100)Set-point weighting: Letting the proportional and derivative actions see only fractions \beta and \gamma of the command, while the integral keeps the full error; it softens the response to command steps (overshoot 22.5 to 1.6 percent with \beta = 0.5) without changing the response to disturbances. Explained in §5.3 Set-point weighting. (FBS §11.5). With \gamma = 0 it contains the derivative on measurement of Section 5.2. The integral action keeps the full error e: a steady state needs the area to stop growing, which happens only when the measured heading equals the command, so the heading still ends on the command (FBS §11.5). The weights change only how the loop answers the command; its answer to a push or to sensor noise stays the same (FBS §11.5). Figure 8 turns the robot with \beta = 1, 0.5 and 0. Watch the overshoot shrink as \beta falls, and the turn slow down at \beta = 0.

Figure 8: Set-point weighting on RO-060’s heading PID (derivative on the measurement). The heading command steps from 0 to 0.1 rad at t = 0.5 s (dashed line). Top: the heading for beta = 1 (blue), 0.5 (green) and 0 (orange). Bottom: the torque request, which jumps at the step by 0.02, 0.01 and 0 N m. Overshoot 22.5, 1.6 and 0.3 percent; within 2 percent of the command 11.11, 5.42 and 9.53 s after the step. Every run ends on 0.1 rad. The last frame holds all 14 s





























\betaJump of the request at the stepOvershootWithin 2 percent after10.02 N·m22.5 percent11.11 s0.50.01 N·m1.6 percent5.42 s00 N·m0.3 percent9.53 s
With \beta = 0.5 the heading reaches 0.1 rad with only 1.6 percent overshoot and settles fastest; with \beta = 0 only the integral action drives the turn, which is smooth but slow. Feedback Systems shows the same on a car’s cruise control: \beta = 1 overshoots and nearly saturates the throttle, \beta = 0 does not overshoot (FBS §11.5, Fig. 11.13). The two changes answer the second problem of the Overview: derivative on measurement removes the 2.85 N·m kick, and \beta = 0.5 cuts the overshoot from 22.5 to 1.6 percent.
In real stacks. PX4’s rate controller puts the derivative action on the feedback path “in order to avoid an effect known as the derivative kick” (PX4 tuning guide). Its code, rate_control.cpp, computes the torque as the proportional action on the rate error, plus the integral, minus the D gain times the measured angular acceleration, plus a feedforward of the rate command. The ros2_control PID, in control_toolbox’s pid.cpp, computes the derivative from the error, (error − last error)/dt, unless the caller passes the error’s rate itself, so a jump in the command kicks unless the caller supplies -dy/dt. Apollo’s PID does the same, diff = (error - previous_error_) / dt in its pid_controller.cc, with the derivative gain 0 in the shipped speed controller. The command no longer kicks; the measurement still does.
6. Filters inside the loop



                  Key point 



The derivative action amplifies whatever jumps in the measurement. A derivative filter (G-3101)Derivative filter: A first-order low-pass on the PID’s derivative path with time constant T_f = T_d/N, N from 5 to 20; it smooths sensor noise such as encoder ticks before the derivative amplifies it, at the cost of some phase margin. Explained in §6.1 The derivative filter. smooths it at the cost of some lag, and a notch filter (G-3102)Notch filter: A filter that blocks a narrow band around one frequency and passes the rest, (s^2 + \omega_0^2)/(s^2 + (\omega_0/Q)s + \omega_0^2); it removes a known vibration, such as a frame’s 10 Hz bending seen by the gyro, with far less lag at the loop’s own frequencies than a low-pass. Explained in §6.2 The notch filter. removes one known frequency, such as the frame’s 10 Hz shaking, with almost no lag elsewhere.


6.1 The derivative filter
The third problem of the Overview comes from the measurement itself. Section 5 took the heading as exact; the robot measures it with its wheel encoders. With:

r_w the wheel radius, 0.05 m, of the differential-drive robotExplained in: Pose and the Differential Drive, §3.1 Why two wheels and a caster;
N_e the encoder’s ticks per wheel turn, 1024;
L the distance between the wheels, 0.2 m;
h the sample period, 0.01 s;
k_d the derivative gain, 0.283 N·m·s per rad, and y the measured heading, in rad;

one tick of one wheel is a travel of:
2\pi \times 0.05 / 1024 = 0.3068 \text{ mm}
2\pi\thinspace r_w / N_e
which turns the measured heading by:
0.0003068 / 0.2 = 1.534 \text{ mrad}
Driving straight at 0.5 m/s, the two wheels tick at slightly different moments, so the measured heading flickers by one tick, 1.53 mrad, while the true heading hardly moves. The derivative on measurement turns one tick within one sample into a rate and a derivative action of:
0.00153 / 0.01 = 0.153 \text{ rad/s}
0.283 \times 0.153 = 0.043 \text{ N·m}
a fifth of the 0.2 N·m limit, in one sample, and back again at the next tick. The motors receive a train of torque spikes for no real motion.
The fix smooths the rate before it is used, with a low-pass filter: a filter that lets slow changes through and smooths fast ones. A first-order low-pass with time constant T_f covers 63 percent of a step of its input in T_f seconds. Put on the derivative path, it is the derivative filter (G-3101)Derivative filter: A first-order low-pass on the PID’s derivative path with time constant T_f = T_d/N, N from 5 to 20; it smooths sensor noise such as encoder ticks before the derivative amplifies it, at the cost of some phase margin. Explained in §6.1 The derivative filter. (FBS §11.5). With:

D the derivative action after the filter, in N·m;
T_f the filter’s time constant, in s;
N a number between 5 and 20 that sets T_f (FBS §11.5);
T_d the derivative time of Section 2.2, 1.415 s;

the filtered derivative action follows the raw one with the lag T_f (FBS §11.5):
T_f\thinspace\frac{dD}{dt} + D = -k_d\thinspace\frac{dy}{dt}
T_f = T_d / N
With N = 10:
T_f = 1.415 / 10 = 0.1415 \text{ s}
A tick lasts one sample, 0.01 s, a fourteenth of T_f, so the filter lets through only a small part of it. As a transfer function (G-2999)Transfer function (control): The number a linear system multiplies an exponential input e^(st) by, written as a function of s, such as G(s) = 1/(8s + 2) for the robot’s speed and 10/s² for its heading; it turns a differential equation into a fraction, so systems can be combined and read by algebra. Explained in: Transfer Functions, Poles and Zeros, §2.1 An exponential goes in, the same exponential comes out, the ideal derivative is k_d times s, and the filtered one (FBS §11.5) is:
\frac{k_d\thinspace s}{1 + s\thinspace T_f}
Figure 9 drives the robot straight for one second. Watch each red spike of the unfiltered derivative action and the small blue bump the filter makes of it.

Figure 9: The derivative filter. The robot drives straight at 0.5 m/s and the heading PID holds the heading at 0. Top: the measured heading in mrad (black), which flickers by one encoder tick, 1.53 mrad, while the true heading (grey dots) rises to only 0.72 mrad. Bottom: the derivative action. Red, unfiltered: every tick makes a spike of 0.043 N m up and down. Blue, filtered with the time constant T_f = 0.1415 s (N = 10): it stays under 0.003 N m. The torque limit, 0.2 N m, lies far above the chart. Time runs frame by frame over 1 s
The filter is not free: it delays the derivative action, and the derivative action is what gives the heading loop its phase margin (G-3045)Phase margin: How much extra phase lag the loop can take at its gain crossover frequency before the closed loop becomes unstable, 180° + \angle L(j\omega_{gc}); typical designs keep 30° to 60°. Explained in: The Nyquist Criterion and Stability Margins, §6.2 Phase margin, the extra lag it can take before it oscillates. The choice of N trades the two (notebook):



































NT_fSwing of the derivative actionPhase marginno filter0±0.043 N·m75°200.071 s±0.0054 N·m64°100.14 s±0.0030 N·m54°50.28 s±0.0016 N·m39°
With N = 10 the spikes shrink from 0.043 to under 0.003 N·m, and 54° of phase margin remains. The encoder ticks are handled; the faster, regular disturbance of the next section needs a different filter.
6.2 The notch filter
A gyro measures the turn rate directly, so the derivative action can take minus the derivative gain k_d, 0.283 N·m·s per rad, times the gyro’s reading: no differencing, and no encoder ticks. PX4’s drones feed their turn-rate control from the gyro in the same way (PX4 controller diagrams). But the robot’s frame bends back and forth at 10 Hz when the motors shake it, and the gyro sees that bending as a turn rate of ±0.3 rad/s. The derivative action passes it on:
0.283 \times 0.3 = 0.085 \text{ N·m}
a 10 Hz torque swing of over 40 percent of the 0.2 N·m limit. Motors driven like this run hot (PX4 filter tuning).
A low-pass filter strong enough for this must sit far below 10 Hz. The heading loop does its work around its gain crossover frequency (G-3042)Gain crossover frequency \omega_{gc}: The frequency at which the loop transfer function’s gain is 1 (0 dB), such as 2.08 rad/s for the robot’s heading loop; the phase margin and the delay margin are read there. Explained in: The Nyquist Criterion and Stability Margins, §2.3 A sine that comes back unchanged, 0.46 Hz, where its loop gain is 1. A low-pass at 1 Hz lets through a tenth of the 10 Hz shaking but lags 24° at 0.46 Hz, and the phase margin falls from 75° to 52°. PX4’s documentation makes the same point: without the notch filter, “you’d have to set the low pass filter cutoff much lower (increasing the phase lag)” (PX4 filter tuning).
The shaking has one frequency, so a filter can remove that frequency alone, the way a good ear tunes out the hum of a fridge and still hears every word. A filter that blocks a narrow band around one frequency and passes the rest is a notch filter (G-3102)Notch filter: A filter that blocks a narrow band around one frequency and passes the rest, (s^2 + \omega_0^2)/(s^2 + (\omega_0/Q)s + \omega_0^2); it removes a known vibration, such as a frame’s 10 Hz bending seen by the gyro, with far less lag at the loop’s own frequencies than a low-pass. Explained in §6.2 The notch filter. (PX4 filter tuning). With:

\omega_0 the angular frequency of the shaking, in rad/s;
Q the sharpness of the notch, the centre frequency over the width of the band;
s the variable of the transfer function (G-2999)Transfer function (control): The number a linear system multiplies an exponential input e^(st) by, written as a function of s, such as G(s) = 1/(8s + 2) for the robot’s speed and 10/s² for its heading; it turns a differential equation into a fraction, so systems can be combined and read by algebra. Explained in: Transfer Functions, Poles and Zeros, §2.1 An exponential goes in, the same exponential comes out, which becomes i\omega for a wave of angular frequency \omega in the frequency response (G-3020)Frequency response: How a stable linear system answers a sine at each rate ω once its start-up swing has died away: the gain and the phase, equal to the length and angle of its transfer function at s = jω; it tells how much of a wave of each rate gets through and how late. Explained in: Frequency Response and Bode Plots, §2.2 Gain and phase: the frequency response.

For the 10 Hz shaking and a band 5 Hz wide:
\omega_0 = 2\pi \times 10 = 62.8 \text{ rad/s}
Q = 10 / 5 = 2
The notch is:
F(s) = \frac{s^2 + \omega_0^2}{s^2 + (\omega_0/Q)\thinspace s + \omega_0^2}
Checks at easy frequencies. For a wave of angular frequency \omega, the square s^2 becomes minus \omega squared. A steady signal has \omega = 0, so every s term vanishes:
F = \frac{\omega_0^2}{\omega_0^2} = 1
The steady part passes unchanged. The shaking has \omega = \omega_0, and the top becomes:
-\omega_0^2 + \omega_0^2 = 0
so the 10 Hz shaking is blocked completely. For a very fast wave the s^2 terms outgrow the rest, top and bottom alike, so F is close to 1 again.
