The two filters answer the third problem of the Overview. The derivative filter cuts the encoder spikes from 0.043 to under 0.003 N·m and keeps 54° of phase margin; on the gyro, the notch removes the 10 Hz swing of 0.085 N·m and keeps 74°, where a low-pass would leave 52°.
In real stacks. PX4 cleans its gyro in a fixed order: a notch filter (parameters IMU_GYRO_NF0_FRQ and IMU_GYRO_NF0_BW, the centre and the width), then a low-pass (IMU_GYRO_CUTOFF), and the derivative action gets its own extra low-pass (IMU_DGYRO_CUTOFF), because “the D-term is most susceptible to noise” (PX4 controller diagrams; PX4 filter tuning). Its static notch is meant for narrow-band noise, “for example a bending mode of the airframe” (PX4 filter tuning). Autoware’s speed controller low-passes its velocity error before the PID (lpf_vel_error_gain: 0.9 in its parameter file). Each of these filters runs as a few lines of code at the controller’s sample rate, like everything else in this Note.
7. Summary








































ProblemFixResult on the robotwhich gainsZiegler–Nichols: raise k_p to k_c, read T_c, use the tablek_c = 200, T_c = 0.40 s; PI gains 90 and 270: fastest, but 0.032 s of delay marginwhich gainsbump test, FOTD model, model-based ruleK = 0.5, \tau = 0.094 s, T = 4.00 s; gains 30.1 and 27.9: drop 0.039 m/s, back in 2.3 s, 0.27 s of delay margincommand step kicksderivative on measurement (\gamma = 0)no 2.85 N·m spikeovershoot after a stepset-point weighting, \beta = 0.5overshoot 22.5 → 1.6 percentencoder ticksderivative filter, T_f = T_d/N, N = 10spikes 0.043 → under 0.003 N·m, phase margin 54°10 Hz frame shakingnotch filter at 10 Hzswing 0.085 → under 0.001 N·m, phase margin 74°

On the 1° ramp, the bump-test gains (30.1 and 27.9) cut the speed drop from 0.054 to 0.039 m/s and the recovery from 5.2 to 2.3 s, because they react harder while keeping a delay margin of 0.27 s; Ziegler–Nichols’s 90 and 270 recover in 0.8 s but oscillate with 0.04 s more delay.
The parallel and standard forms are one controller (the integral gain is k_p over T_i, the derivative gain k_p times T_d), so a tuner can work with two times that compare directly with the robot’s time constants.
The ideal speed model never oscillates, but the 20-sample average makes the measured speed 0.095 s late, so the real loop oscillates at k_c = 200 with period 0.40 s, where the lags add up to half a period.
Ziegler–Nichols’s table turns k_c and T_c into gains from one experiment, so it is quick, but it sees one point of the loop and gives fragile loops (FBS §11.3).
A bump test gives K, \tau and T, which match the drag, the mass and the averaging lag, so a rule built for this model can use all three and gives a third of the Ziegler–Nichols gain.
The derivative of a command step is a spike (2.85 N·m, 14 times the limit), so the derivative acts on the measurement, which changes nothing between steps.
The proportional action also jumps at a step, so weighting the command by \beta = 0.5 cuts the overshoot from 22.5 to 1.6 percent while the integral, on the full error, still reaches the command.
The derivative action turns each 1.53 mrad encoder tick into a 0.043 N·m spike, so a low-pass whose time constant is the derivative time over N smooths it, at the price of phase margin (75° to 54° with N = 10).
A notch removes one frequency, so it blocks the frame’s 10 Hz shaking with a lag of 1.4° at the loop’s 0.46 Hz, where a 1 Hz low-pass would lag 24° and leave a phase margin of 52°.

The robot now has tuned gains and a PID that neither kicks nor chatters. Everything in this Note ran on a computer that reads the sensors and updates the commands every 0.01 s: the average, the derivative as a difference over one sample, the filters as a few lines of code. What that sampling does to a controller designed as a continuous law, and how to choose the sample rate, is the subject of digital controlExplained in: Digital Control: Sampling a Continuous Controller, §1. Overview.
