actions \beta and \gamma of the command, while the integral keeps the full error; it softens the response to command steps (overshoot 22.5 to 1.6 percent with \beta = 0.5) without changing the response to disturbances. Explained in §5.3 Set-point weighting.Letting the proportional and derivative actions see only fractions \beta and \gamma of the command, while the integral keeps the full error; it softens the response to command steps (overshoot 22.5 to 1.6 percent with \beta = 0.5) without changing the response to disturbances.Derivative filter (G-3101)Derivative filter: A first-order low-pass on the PID’s derivative path with time constant T_f = T_d/N, N from 5 to 20; it smooths sensor noise such as encoder ticks before the derivative amplifies it, at the cost of some phase margin. Explained in §6.1 The derivative filter.A first-order low-pass on the PID’s derivative path with time constant T_f = T_d/N, N from 5 to 20; it smooths sensor noise such as encoder ticks before the derivative amplifies it, at the cost of some phase margin.Notch filter (G-3102)Notch filter: A filter that blocks a narrow band around one frequency and passes the rest, (s^2 + \omega_0^2)/(s^2 + (\omega_0/Q)s + \omega_0^2); it removes a known vibration, such as a frame’s 10 Hz bending seen by the gyro, with far less lag at the loop’s own frequencies than a low-pass. Explained in §6.2 The notch filter.A filter that blocks a narrow band around one frequency and passes the rest, (s^2 + \omega_0^2)/(s^2 + (\omega_0/Q)s + \omega_0^2); it removes a known vibration, such as a frame’s 10 Hz bending seen by the gyro, with far less lag at the loop’s own frequencies than a low-pass.Ideal PID controller (G-3090)Ideal PID controller: The textbook PID law, u = k_p e + k_i \int e\thinspace dt + k_d\thinspace de/dt, with nothing added: no output limits, no filters, every action on the error; a real controller adds set-point weights, derivative on measurement, filters and anti-windup to it. Explained in §2.1 