nal action (2 s for k_p = 20, k_i = 10), so a short one makes the integral take over quickly.Derivative time T_d (G-3104)Derivative time T_d: The derivative gain over the proportional gain, T_d = k_d/k_p, in seconds: how far ahead the derivative action predicts the error along a straight line, since it applies now the change the proportional action will see T_d later. Explained in §2.2 The standard form: one gain and two times.The derivative gain over the proportional gain, T_d = k_d/k_p, in seconds: how far ahead the derivative action predicts the error along a straight line, since it applies now the change the proportional action will see T_d later.Critical gain and critical period (G-3094)Critical gain and critical period: The proportional gain k_c at which a loop oscillates without growing or dying, and the period T_c of that oscillation (200 N per m/s and 0.40 s for the robot’s speed loop); they mark the point where the loop’s lags add up to half a period, and the Ziegler–Nichols table uses them. Also called ultimate gain and period. Explained in §3.3 Raise the gain until the loop oscillates.The proportional gain k_c at which a loop oscillates without growing or dying, and the period T_c of that oscillation (200 N per m/s and 0.40 s for the robot’s speed loop); they mark the point where the loop’s lags add up to half a period, and the Ziegler–Nichols table uses them. Also called ultimate gain and period.Bump test (G-3095)Bump test: An open-loop experiment: hold the process steady, step its input once (the drive force from 0 to 1 N) and record the output until it settles; its curve gives the gain, the apparent delay and the time constant used to tune a controller. Explained in §4.1 The bump test.An open-loop experiment: hold the process steady, step its input once (the drive force from 0 to 1 N) and record the output until it settles; its curve gives the gain, the apparent delay and the time constant used to tune a controller.First-order-plus-time-delay (FOTD) m