ega; used to predict motion at low speed. Explained in: Pose and the Differential Drive, §5.2 The kinematic modelEquations that give how a robot’s configuration changes from its speed inputs alone, ignoring masses and forces, such as \dot{x} = v\cos\theta, \dot{y} = v\sin\theta, \dot{\theta} = \omega; used to predict motion at low speed.Pose (G-2280)Pose: Where a robot is and which way it faces: on a floor, its position (x, y) and heading \theta, such as (2 m, 1 m, 30°); needed because a robot at one spot drives off differently depending on its heading. Explained in: Pose and the Differential Drive, §2.2 Why position is not enough: the headingWhere a robot is and which way it faces: on a floor, its position (x, y) and heading \theta, such as (2 m, 1 m, 30°); needed because a robot at one spot drives off differently depending on its heading.Linear velocity v (G-2291)Linear velocity v: How fast a robot’s reference point (for a differential drive, the middle of the axle) moves along its heading, in m/s; for a differential drive, the average of the two wheel speeds, so it sets how fast the robot advances. Explained in: Pose and the Differential Drive, §4.1 Why we describe motion by linear and angular velocityHow fast a robot’s reference point (for a differential drive, the middle of the axle) moves along its heading, in m/s; for a differential drive, the average of the two wheel speeds, so it sets how fast the robot advances.Angular velocity \omega (G-2292)Angular velocity \omega: How fast a robot’s heading changes, in rad/s, positive to the left; for a differential drive, the difference of the wheel speeds divided by the wheel separation. Explained in: Pose and the Differential Drive, §4.1 Why we describe motion by linear and angular velocityHow fast a robot’s heading changes, in rad/s, positive to the left; for a differential drive, the difference of the wheel speeds divided by the wheel separation.Dead reckoning (G-2311)Dead reckoning: Working out a robot’s position from a known start by adding up its own moves, without looking at its surroundings; simple, but its error grows with every move, so it needs readings to correct it. Explained in: Why a Robot Is Never Sure: State, Controls and Measurements, §2.1 Why counting moves is not enough: dead reckoningWorking out a robot’s position from a known start by adding up its own moves, without looking at its surroundings; simple, but its error grows with every move, so it needs readings to correct it.Standard deviation of a random variable (G-1870)Standard deviation of a random variable: The square root of a random variable’s variance, in the units of X; it says how far values typically fall from the expected value. Explained in: Random Variables as Functions, Expected Value and Variance, §4.1 Variance term by termThe square root of a random variable’s variance, in the units of X; it says how far values typically fall from the expected value.Gaussian distribution (G-827)Gaussian distribution: Another name for the normal distribution: the symmetric, bell-shaped continuous distribution set by its mean and standard deviation, used to model many measurements. Explained in: The Normal Distribution, §2. What the normal distribution isAnother name for the normal distribution: the symmetric, bell-shaped continuous distribution set by its mean and standard deviation, used to model many measurements.Probability density (G-1569)Probability density: The height of a continuous distribution’s curve; compares how likely nearby values are. Explained in: Probability Density Function and the CDF of a Continuous Variable, §5. What the density at a point meansThe height of a continuous distribution’s curve; compares how likely nearby values are.Rolling without slipping (G-2290)Rolling without slipping: A wheel moving so that its contact point does not skid: it moves forward by its radius times the angle it turns and never slides sideways, which is what lets wheel turns predict motion. Explained in: Pose and the Differential Drive, §3.2 Why wheel spin gives ground speed: rolling without slippingA wheel moving so that its contact point does not skid: it moves forward by its radius times the angle it turns and never slides sideways, which is what lets wheel turns predict motion.Forward kinematics (wheeled robot) (G-2294)Forward kinematics (wheeled robot): Working out the robot’s linear and angular velocity from its wheel speeds, used to predict where the robot goes. Explained in: Pose and the Differential Drive, §4.3 Wheel speeds to linear and angular velocityWorking out the robot’s linear and angular velocity from its wheel speeds, used to predict where the robot goes.Euler step (G-2299)Euler step: One step of predicting motion: hold the rates fixed for a short time \Delta t and add rate times \Delta t to each quantity; repeated, it traces a path, more accurately the smaller \Delta t is. Explained in: Pose and the Differential Drive, §5.3 Why we predict the path step by stepOne step of predicting motion: hold the rates fixed for a short time \Delta t and add rate times \Delta t to each quantity; repeated, it traces a path, more accurately the smaller \Delta t is.Independent events (G-934)Independent events: Events where one happening does not change the probability of the other; for them the probability that both happen is the product of their probabilities. Explained in: Naive Bayes Foundations: Independent Events, §2. The definitionEvents where one happening does not change the probability of the other; for them the probability that both happen is the product of their probabilities.Instantaneous centre of curvature (ICC) (G-2529)Instantaneous centre of curvature (ICC): The point a turning robot’s whole body circles at a given moment; for a differential drive it lies on the axle line at distance R = v/\omega from the midpoint, so it fixes how fast each wheel must move. Explained in: Pose and the Differential Drive, §4.2 Why the robot turns about a point on its axle